<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matthew III Csák</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1260–1321</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matthew III Csák was a Hungarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> who built a vast domain in the north-west of the kingdom, largely in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, as royal power collapsed around 1300. From his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trenčín</a> he ruled almost as an independent prince. He resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a>, whose victory at Rozgony in 1312 weakened his allies, and his domain broke up after his death in 1321.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matthew-iii-csak.jpg" alt="Matthew III Csák" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16th_century_portrait_of_Matthew_III_Cs%C3%A1k.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…grandson of Andrew II born in Venice, faced rival claimants backed by the papacy and Naples, and real power in many regions passed to oligarchs such as <b>Matthew Csák</b> in the north-west, Amadeus Aba in the north-east, Ladislaus Kán in Transylvania and the Kőszegi family in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time much of the country was controlled by great lords, the so-called oligarchs, who ruled their regions as independent princes: <b>Matthew Csák</b> in the north-west, Amadeus Aba in the north-east, Ladislaus Kán in Transylvania and the Kőszegi family in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Matthew III Csák</b>, of a leading baronial clan, built up power in the Váh valley in the 1290s as royal authority collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/domain-of-matthew-csak">Domain of Matthew Csák</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trenčín</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trenčín</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.89,18.04&amp;year=1299" aria-label="Trenčín on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4967 457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matthew III Csák"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5417 727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5417 727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.89,18.04&amp;year=1299">What was here in 1299? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
